---
intent: >
  Broaden `mcpServerConfig.args` from `string[]` to `(string | null)[]` across
  the parser's central type surface and runtime behavior. The parser must
  preserve YAML null entries verbatim — same type, same index — through both
  validation and the `interpolateArgs` helper, removing the prior
  string-only-arg validation rule and any retired `<BRAIN_PATH>`-style
  literal-sentinel awareness. Update the test suite to cover null-armed
  parsing, null passthrough through `interpolateArgs`, and structural
  round-trip from YAML through to the typed result. Strict scope: changes
  land only in `src/service/parseBrainAide/` (orchestrator + tests) and the
  `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` type in `src/types/index.ts`. Consumer modules
  (sync, buildBrainState, provisionBrain, cli/init, brain tool) have their
  own plans and consume the broadened type via downstream architects'
  scopes.
---

## Project Structure

This plan is scoped to a single orchestrator with one inline helper, two
exports, and one co-located test file. The 4→6 marker rework is fully done
(plan.aide is 32/32 from the prior iteration); the only changes this plan
delivers are the args-type broadening to `(string | null)[]` and the
behaviors that follow from it. The folder shape, the orchestrator/helper
layout, and the single-helper-exception placement (per the spec's
"Single-helper file layout" Strategy paragraph) all stay unchanged.

```
src/service/parseBrainAide/
├── .aide                    ← intent spec for this orchestrator (existing — body just rewritten in Stages 1+3)
├── plan.aide                ← this file (existing — being rewritten for the args broadening)
├── index.ts                 ← orchestrator (existing — modified)
│                              MARKER_PAIRS: unchanged (six pairs, fixed order)
│                              extractMarkerSections(body: string)
│                                : { kind: "ok"; orientation: string; config: string; playbookIndex: string;
│                                    studyPlaybook: string; updatePlaybook: string; researchIndex: string }
│                                | { kind: "malformed-body"; reason: string }    (unchanged)
│                              interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): (string | null)[]
│                                (modified — element-walk visits each entry; string elements get
│                                 ${name}-style placeholder substitution; null elements pass through
│                                 unchanged at their original index)
│                              parseBrainAideFromString(content: string): ParseBrainAideResult
│                                (modified — the args validation predicate is broadened from
│                                 "every element is a string" to "every element is a string OR null";
│                                 the parsed args array is forwarded as `(string | null)[]`)
│                              parseBrainAide(root: string): Promise<ParseBrainAideResult>
│                                (default export, signature unchanged — returns the same union with
│                                 the broadened args type on the ok variant)
└── index.test.ts            ← co-located vitest suite (existing — modified)
                               Adds null-armed parsing cases (single null, multiple nulls, all nulls,
                               null-only mixed with strings) for both parseBrainAide (disk path) and
                               parseBrainAideFromString (in-memory path)
                               Adds interpolateArgs null-passthrough cases (null only, null + ${name},
                               null preserved at original index)
                               Adds an args-type-contract round-trip case proving null entries flow
                               from source YAML byte → JS null → typed `(string | null)[]` value at the
                               same index, with no compaction or coercion
                               Removes any test asserting that null entries are rejected as
                               malformed-frontmatter (none exist today, but the audit step verifies)
                               Removes any test that pattern-matches a retired literal-string sentinel
                               (`<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, etc.) as a parse-level signal (none exist
                               today, but the audit step verifies)
```

## Prerequisites

This plan touches one file outside the parser's scope. It is the same
cross-cutting touchpoint precedent the prior 4→6 plan used (see Decisions);
the contract is bounded to one type and one JSDoc block.

- **`src/types/index.ts`** — must export `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` such
  that its `args` field is typed `(string | null)[]`. Concretely the type
  becomes:
  ```
  export type BrainAideMcpServerConfig = {
    command: string;
    args: (string | null)[];
  };
  ```
  `BrainAideConfig` automatically inherits the broadened arg type because
  it embeds `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` directly. The `ParseBrainAideResult.ok`
  variant's `mcpServerConfig: BrainAideMcpServerConfig` field carries the
  same broadened shape with no further edit needed at that site. The
  separate `McpServerEntry` type used for `.mcp.json` writes (consumed by
  `writeMcpEntry`, `buildBrainState`'s drift comparison, and
  `provisionBrain`'s MCP step) STAYS `args: string[]` — that type
  represents the post-sync byte-faithful shape, and sync's null-refusal
  precondition means `McpServerEntry` only ever carries fully-resolved
  string args. The two types intentionally diverge at the parse-vs-write
  boundary.

  This edit's reach into `src/types/` is bounded: only `BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args`
  changes, plus the JSDoc on that type and on `BrainAideConfig` to reflect
  the null-as-unwired-slot contract. Nothing else in `src/types/index.ts`
  is modified — `McpServerEntry`, `BrainState`, `BrainToolResult`,
  `ParseBrainAideResult`, and every other type stay byte-identical.

## Plan

### 1. Broaden `BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args` to `(string | null)[]` in `src/types/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [ ] Update the `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` type so its `args` field is typed `(string | null)[]`. Concrete type after edit:
  ```
  export type BrainAideMcpServerConfig = {
    command: string;
    args: (string | null)[];
  };
  ```
  Field order, location in the file, and surrounding code stay byte-identical — the only character changes are the union widening on `args`.
- [ ] Update the JSDoc block on `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` and on `BrainAideConfig` to reflect the null-as-unwired-slot contract. The JSDoc must say in prose that:
  - `args` is `(string | null)[]`; every element is either a fully-formed argument string OR YAML null.
  - YAML null at any index is the explicit unwired-slot signal — replacing the retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal-sentinel design.
  - The parser preserves null entries verbatim at their original indexes; downstream consumers (sync's null-refusal, `buildBrainState`'s null-detection, `interpolateArgs`'s null-passthrough) branch on null structurally.
  - Every retired-vocabulary mention of `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, magic UUIDs, or any other string sentinel MUST be removed from this JSDoc if any reference exists. Do NOT name them in this JSDoc as live signals; if naming the retired tokens is needed for migration prose, reference the spec's strict-failure-migration outcome instead of repeating the names here.
- [ ] Do NOT touch `McpServerEntry` — it remains `{ command: string; args: string[] }`. The two types intentionally diverge: parse-time accepts `(string | null)[]`; post-sync `.mcp.json` writes accept only `string[]`. Any consumer that needs to convert from `(string | null)[]` to `string[]` (e.g. sync after null-refusal passes) does so in its own consumer plan, not here.
- [ ] Do NOT touch `BrainAideConfig`'s shape — it embeds `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` and inherits the broadened type automatically. Do NOT touch `ParseBrainAideResult` — its `ok` variant carries `mcpServerConfig: BrainAideMcpServerConfig` and inherits the same broadened type.
- [ ] Run `npx tsc --noEmit` after the edit. Expected: red errors in `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` (the `parseBrainAideFromString` validator currently rejects non-string args, and the `interpolateArgs` helper currently calls `arg.replace(...)` on every element which only typechecks against `string`); red errors in any consumer that constructs an args array as `string[]` and assigns to a `BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args` slot. The parser itself will be fixed in Step 2; consumers are out of scope. Do NOT fix consumer modules (`src/service/install/provisionBrain/`, `src/service/buildBrainState/`, `src/cli/sync/`, `src/cli/init/`, `src/tools/brain/`) in this step or any other step in this plan — their own plans will catch the type errors and migrate them.

### 2. Broaden the parser's args validation and `interpolateArgs` helper to accept and preserve null entries in `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

This step is a single coupled unit — the args validation predicate inside `parseBrainAideFromString`, the `interpolateArgs` helper's element walk, and the JSDoc on both functions must change together so the file compiles cleanly and the runtime behavior matches the broadened type contract in one edit. Lettered sub-steps run in one implementor session.

- [ ] 2a. Locate the args validation predicate inside `parseBrainAideFromString` (currently the line that reads `if (!Array.isArray(mcpServerConfig["args"]) || !mcpServerConfig["args"].every((a) => typeof a === "string"))` and the surrounding malformed-frontmatter return). Broaden the element predicate from `typeof a === "string"` to accept either string OR null. Concretely the predicate becomes "every element of `args` is either a string or null"; any other element type (number, boolean, object, undefined) still returns `malformed-frontmatter` with a reason naming the field. Per the spec's Strategy paragraph "Schema is exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` with `args` typed `(string | null)[]`; deprecated fields are rejected, not stripped" — null entries are part of the contract, not malformed. Update the malformed-frontmatter reason string for non-string-non-null elements to read "mcpServerConfig.args is required and must be an array of strings or null" (or equivalent — the rule is to surface the contract, including null, in the error so users understand what is allowed).
- [ ] 2b. Update the `parsedFrontmatter.mcpServerConfig.args` cast site from `mcpServerConfig["args"] as string[]` to `mcpServerConfig["args"] as (string | null)[]`. The runtime YAML parser already produces JS `null` for bare `-` entries; the only edit is the type assertion that flows the broadened element type into `BrainAideConfig.mcpServerConfig.args`. Preserve every existing index — no compaction, no filtering, no reordering.
- [ ] 2c. Rewrite `interpolateArgs`'s element walk so the helper visits each entry by structure rather than calling `.replace(...)` blindly. The new behavior is per-element: (i) if the entry is `null`, pass it through unchanged at the same index; (ii) if the entry is a string, apply the existing `${fieldName}` placeholder substitution against the top-level frontmatter substitutions map (today only `${name}` is resolvable). The return-type annotation broadens from `string[]` to `(string | null)[]`. The `substitutions` map and the placeholder regex stay byte-identical. The array length stays equal to the input args length — null entries do NOT compact away. Per the spec's "Substitution is exposed as a separate helper" Strategy paragraph and the "interpolateArgs against a null-armed config preserves the null at its index" Good example, the helper is the third party in the null-passthrough contract (alongside sync's null-refusal and buildBrainState's null-detection); coercing the null to empty string here would silently break sync's null-refusal downstream.
- [ ] 2d. Update the JSDoc above `interpolateArgs` to reflect the new contract:
  - The return type is `(string | null)[]`, not `string[]`.
  - The helper walks args element-by-element. String elements get `${fieldName}` placeholder substitution; null elements pass through unchanged at their original index because there is no string to substitute into.
  - The default scaffold contains no `${...}` placeholders and may carry one or more nulls at unwired-slot indexes; for that scaffold the helper is structurally a no-op (output deep-equals input).
  - Audience clarification: this is the `.mcp.json`-writer surface, so consumers running `interpolateArgs` and then sync handle null-refusal in sync, never in this helper. The interpolation surface is the ONLY substitution surface in the package and never runs against any of the six body sections.
  - Remove any retired-vocabulary references in the JSDoc (e.g. mentions of `<BRAIN_PATH>`-style sentinels as live signals). If any retired-name reference exists today, scrub it; the rework retired the literal-sentinel design entirely.
- [ ] 2e. Update the JSDoc above `parseBrainAideFromString` to mention the broadened args contract. Specifically the "Step 4: Validate required fields" comment block must say that `mcpServerConfig.args` is an array whose every element is string OR null; null is part of the contract, NOT a malformed value. The validation order (name → mcpServerConfig (object) → mcpServerConfig.command → mcpServerConfig.args) stays unchanged. Update the "Step 4b: Reject deprecated fields" comment block to keep its existing four-name list (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`) — null in args is NOT a deprecated field; the broadening is at the type level, not the validation set.
- [ ] 2f. Update the JSDoc above the default-export `parseBrainAide` to reflect the args contract. The "Load-bearing invariants" block must mention that `mcpServerConfig.args` is `(string | null)[]` and that null entries are preserved verbatim at their original indexes. The "Never throws" and "Never interpolates any body section" invariants stay byte-identical. Add one line stating that null is the explicit unwired-slot signal and that there is no literal-string sentinel (`<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, magic UUIDs) recognized at any layer.
- [ ] 2g. Audit the entire `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` file for any remaining reference to a literal-string sentinel as a parse-level signal. Search for `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, `__UNWIRED__`, and any `<` followed by uppercase identifier-shape text matching `[A-Z_]+>` in identifiers, comments, or string constants. Per the spec's "A parser that pattern-matches `<BRAIN_PATH>` (or any other literal-string sentinel)" undesired outcome and the Strategy paragraph "YAML null is the unwired-slot signal", every such reference must be either removed (if it was active code) or surrounded by a "retired-vocabulary, do not re-add" comment block (if naming it serves as a forbidden-token reference for future maintainers). Today's code does not branch on any string sentinel — the audit verifies post-edit that no sentinel reference snuck in via the JSDoc rewrite or the comment updates.
- [ ] 2h. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` after the edits. Expected: green for `src/types/index.ts` and `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`; red errors persist in consumer modules (sync, buildBrainState, provisionBrain, cli/init, tools/brain) that consume args as `string[]`. Do NOT fix consumer modules — their own plans will migrate them.

### 3. Add null-handling test cases to `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

These cases land on top of the existing 32-step suite. They do NOT modify any existing case; the prior 4→6 rework already brought the fixtures and helpers to the six-section vocabulary. The new behavior under test is exclusively the args type broadening.

- [ ] 3a. Add a new describe block — name it after the args type contract (e.g. `"args type contract — string | null"`). Inside it, add a happy-path null-armed case:
  - Build a fixture using the `makeCanonicalContent` helper with a frontmatter override that lands a YAML null at one args index. The YAML representation is a bare `-` with no scalar (e.g. `args:\n    - "@example/mcp-launcher"\n    - "D:/brains/my-brain"\n    -`). Since `makeCanonicalContent` does not currently take a custom `argsLines` override, either add an `argsLines` override to the helper (preferred — single small extension that future cases can reuse) OR construct the content string inline for this test (acceptable for one-off cases).
  - Assert `result.kind === "ok"`.
  - Assert `result.mcpServerConfig.args.length === 3` (no compaction).
  - Assert `result.mcpServerConfig.args[0]` and `result.mcpServerConfig.args[1]` are the original string entries (`"@example/mcp-launcher"`, `"D:/brains/my-brain"`).
  - Assert `result.mcpServerConfig.args[2] === null` (JS strict equality, not loose). The null index in the parsed result MUST equal the null index in the source YAML.
  - This test maps directly to the spec's "A null-armed `brain.aide`... parses with a YAML null preserved at the unwired index" Good example.
- [ ] 3b. Add a multi-null parsing case:
  - Frontmatter args list with two YAML nulls at non-adjacent indexes (e.g. positions 1 and 3 of a four-entry args list, with strings at 0 and 2).
  - Assert `result.kind === "ok"`, `result.mcpServerConfig.args.length === 4`, the two string indexes carry their string values, and the two null indexes carry JS `null` (strict equality).
  - This locks in the structural-null invariant: multiple nulls at arbitrary indexes preserve their indexes one-to-one with the source YAML (per the spec's "A parser that drops null entries from `args` or compacts the array" undesired outcome).
- [ ] 3c. Add an all-null parsing case:
  - Frontmatter args list with three entries, all YAML nulls.
  - Assert `result.kind === "ok"`, `result.mcpServerConfig.args.length === 3`, and every element is JS `null` (strict equality on each index, not deep-equal against an array literal — strict equality per index makes the test failure mode pinpoint the offending index).
  - This case may seem extreme but the parser's contract is per-element: every element passes through verbatim, regardless of the mix.
- [ ] 3d. Add a null-rejected-as-malformed REGRESSION-PREVENTION case:
  - Construct a fixture frontmatter args list with a single null entry mixed with strings.
  - Assert `result.kind === "ok"` (NOT `"malformed-frontmatter"`).
  - The test case header explicitly notes that null is part of the args contract, not a malformed value. The case is the regression bumper for the spec's "A parser that rejects null entries in args as `malformed-frontmatter`" undesired outcome.
- [ ] 3e. Add an `interpolateArgs` null-passthrough happy path case (in the existing `3m — interpolateArgs canonical config is a no-op` describe block, OR a new describe block `"4b — interpolateArgs null-passthrough"` if grouping by step is preferred). The test:
  - Construct a `BrainAideConfig` literal with `args: ["a", null, "b"]` and `name: "my-brain"`.
  - Call `interpolateArgs(config)`.
  - Assert the result deep-equals `["a", null, "b"]` (the strings have no `${...}` placeholders, so they pass through; the null at index 1 passes through unchanged).
  - Assert the result has `length === 3` (no compaction).
  - Maps directly to the spec's "interpolateArgs against a null-armed config preserves the null at its index" Good example.
- [ ] 3f. Add an `interpolateArgs` mixed-substitution-and-null case:
  - Construct a `BrainAideConfig` literal with `args: ["some-launcher", "--profile", "${name}", null]` and `name: "dev-brain"`.
  - Call `interpolateArgs(config)`.
  - Assert the result deep-equals `["some-launcher", "--profile", "dev-brain", null]`. Per-element substitution applies to the `${name}` index; null at the next index passes through unchanged at its original position.
  - Maps directly to the spec's "An advanced user opts into interpolation by referencing `${name}`; null entries continue to pass through" Good example.
- [ ] 3g. Add a structural round-trip case (in the new args-type-contract describe block from 3a, or a new sibling block):
  - Build a fixture with a YAML null at args index 3 (the canonical Obsidian-default-position).
  - Call `parseBrainAide` on the fixture; assert `result.kind === "ok"` and `result.mcpServerConfig.args[3] === null`.
  - Call `interpolateArgs(result)` (using the flattened ok variant's `name` and `mcpServerConfig` fields, per the existing 3m'' test pattern).
  - Assert the interpolation result has `length === 4` and `interpolated[3] === null` (strict equality). The structural unwired-slot signal survives the entire parser → interpolateArgs pipeline at the same index.
  - This case proves the spec's "Null passthrough is mandatory and tested at every transformation the parser exposes" Strategy paragraph end-to-end: the parser preserves null, the interpolation helper preserves null, and the index where the null lives in the source YAML is the same index where it lives at every observable point in the pipeline.
- [ ] 3h. Add a parity case linking `parseBrainAideFromString` to `parseBrainAide` for the null-armed input (extend the existing `3n — parseBrainAideFromString parses bytes identically to parseBrainAide from disk` describe block):
  - Build a null-armed content string (the same fixture used in 3a, but constructed once and shared).
  - Write the content to disk and call `parseBrainAide`; call `parseBrainAideFromString` on the same content.
  - Assert both results are deep-equal AND their `mcpServerConfig.args[<null-index>]` is JS `null` on both paths.
  - The parity test catches regressions where one path inadvertently coerces null while the other preserves it (e.g. if a future maintainer adds a "normalize" pass to one path only).
- [ ] 3i. Audit the existing test file for any test that pattern-matches a retired literal-string sentinel (`<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, `__UNWIRED__`, magic-UUID-shaped strings) as a parse-level signal. Specifically grep the file for `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, `__UNWIRED__`, and any `<[A-Z_]+>` shape inside a string fixture or assertion. Per the spec's "A parser that pattern-matches `<BRAIN_PATH>`..." undesired outcome and the Strategy paragraph "YAML null is the unwired-slot signal; ... the parser does NOT recognize `<BRAIN_PATH>`..." — none of these tokens may appear in any active test case as a parse-level signal. Today's tests do not assert on any sentinel; the audit is a regression-prevention pass that confirms post-edit. If an audit hit appears (inside a comment naming the retired vocabulary as forbidden, for example), leave it alone — that's the spec's anti-example carve-out and is the mechanism that forbids the token. Live test fixtures and assertions MUST NOT contain the tokens.
- [ ] 3j. Run `npx vitest run src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts` after every new case lands. Expected: green across the entire suite. Red failures pinpoint either a parser bug (return to Step 2 sub-steps and fix) or a test-construction bug (fix in this step). Do NOT advance until this file is fully green.

### 4. Final compile-and-test sweep over the parser slice

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [ ] Run `npx tsc --noEmit` on the full project. Expected: green across `src/types/index.ts` and `src/service/parseBrainAide/`; red errors in consumer modules (`src/cli/sync/`, `src/service/buildBrainState/`, `src/service/install/provisionBrain/`, `src/cli/init/`, `src/tools/brain/`) that read args as `string[]` are EXPECTED — their plans land next and will migrate them. Do NOT fix consumers in this plan.
- [ ] Run `npx vitest run src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts`. Expected: green. If any test in this file remains red, return to Step 2 or Step 3 and fix the cause; do not advance until this file is fully green.
- [ ] Run `npx vitest run` against the whole repo (no filter). Expected: red in consumer module tests (provisionBrain, brain tool, sync, init, buildBrainState) — same forcing-event shape as the tsc errors. The parser slice's tests stay green. Do NOT fix consumer tests in this plan.
- [ ] Audit `src/service/parseBrainAide/` (the parser file and its test file together) for any leftover `string[]` annotation on an args-shaped slot. Specifically grep the directory for the substring `args: string[]` and `args as string[]`. Every match must be either: replaced with `args: (string | null)[]` / `args as (string | null)[]`; or absent. The Project Structure tree's annotated function signatures (above) match the post-edit state; any deviation in the actual file is a bug to fix. Do NOT edit `McpServerEntry` in `src/types/index.ts` — that type's `args: string[]` annotation is correct and load-bearing for the post-sync byte-faithful boundary.
- [ ] Audit the parser slice for any leftover retired-sentinel token as a live signal. Grep for `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<API_TOKEN>`, `__UNWIRED__`. Each match must be in a forbidden-token comment (per the spec's anti-example carve-out) or absent — never as a live recognized string in code or as a fixture in an active test assertion.

## Decisions

- **Null-passthrough is mandatory at both the parser and the interpolation helper, in one coupled step.** The validation predicate inside `parseBrainAideFromString` and the element walk inside `interpolateArgs` are bundled into Step 2 because they live in the same file, share the same compile-time forcing event (the type rewrite from Step 1), and produce a single coherent unit of behavior change. Splitting them into two separate steps would land an intermediate state where the type allows null but `interpolateArgs` still does `arg.replace(...)` blindly — a runtime crash on the first null entry that would mask the type contract change. The coupled step lets the implementor land the type, the validator, and the helper in one edit and run a single tsc/vitest sweep to confirm the parser slice is internally consistent.

- **`McpServerEntry` stays `args: string[]`; the divergence at the parse-vs-write boundary is intentional.** Per the spec's "Sync writes the post-interpolation `mcpServerConfig` byte-for-byte into `.mcp.json`'s `mcpServers["brain"]` entry; if any arg is null at sync time, sync refuses" outcome, sync's null-refusal precondition is what guarantees `McpServerEntry` only ever carries fully-resolved string args. Broadening `McpServerEntry` to `(string | null)[]` would force every `.mcp.json`-writer in the package to handle null at every call site (writeMcpEntry, the buildBrainState drift comparison, the provisionBrain MCP step) — a regression that defeats the type-system invariant of "type at the boundary names the post-state contract, not every transient state on the way to it". The two types diverge by design: `BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args` is `(string | null)[]` (the parse-time shape, which carries the unwired-slot signal); `McpServerEntry.args` is `string[]` (the post-sync shape, which sync's null-refusal guarantees). Each consumer plan migrates its call site as needed (sync filters/validates before producing an `McpServerEntry`; buildBrainState's drift comparison reads the parsed config's typed null and treats null-bearing args as the no-mcp-entry semantic per the spec's "buildBrainState maps any null entry... to no-mcp-entry" outcome, which is a buildBrainState-plan concern).

- **Cross-cutting type touchpoint stays in this plan, mirroring the prior 4→6 plan's precedent.** Per `plan-aide.md`'s single-module scoping rule, a plan touches only its scope directory. `src/types/index.ts` lives outside `src/service/parseBrainAide/`. Two options: (a) carve out a separate plan.aide for `src/types/` declaring the type shape change as its own work; (b) declare the touchpoint in this plan's Prerequisites section and execute it as Step 1. Chose (b), exactly as the prior 4→6 amendment plan did. Rationale: `src/types/` has no orchestrator, no `.aide` spec, no plan home of its own; the single-field/single-shape edit is tightly coupled to the parser's matching changes (you cannot land the type without the parser, or vice versa, without breaking compile); and forking a plan whose only step is "broaden one type field" introduces orchestration churn without isolating any architectural decision. The Prerequisites section bounds the edit's reach into `src/types/index.ts` to exactly the `BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args` field, the JSDoc on it and on `BrainAideConfig`, and nothing else.

- **`BrainAideConfig` and `ParseBrainAideResult` inherit the broadening automatically.** `BrainAideConfig` embeds `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` directly (no inline arg-type override); its `mcpServerConfig.args` field becomes `(string | null)[]` by transitive type inference the moment `BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args` widens. `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` carries `mcpServerConfig: BrainAideMcpServerConfig` and inherits the same. Step 1's edit is therefore one type edit that propagates to three call sites without explicit edits at the embed points — exactly the central-types invariant the spec's "Types live centrally in `src/types/`" Strategy paragraph mandates.

- **The args validation predicate is broadened, not deleted.** A user who writes `args: 42` (number) or `args: { command: "x" }` (object) still sees `malformed-frontmatter` — the validation is "every element is string OR null", not "any value is acceptable". Per the spec's "Schema is exactly... `args` typed `(string | null)[]`; deprecated fields are rejected, not stripped" outcome, the broadening adds null to the accepted set without weakening the surrounding validation. Other element types (number, boolean, object, undefined) remain malformed. The reason string is updated to surface the "string or null" contract so users understand what the parser allows.

- **The `interpolateArgs` element walk is per-element, not per-string-then-rejoin.** The current implementation calls `arg.replace(...)` on every element of the args array — which only typechecks against `string` and crashes at runtime against `null`. The fix is structural: visit each element by type (string → substitute; null → passthrough). The existing `${(\w+)}` regex, the `substitutions` map, and the `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call` lookup all stay byte-identical inside the string branch. The array length and element indexes stay equal between input and output — interpolation is a per-string transform, never an array-shape transform. Per the spec's "Per-element interpolation composes cleanly with per-element null passthrough" Good example, the helper's contract is per-element substitution within the `(string | null)[]` shape.

- **Tests for the new behavior land as additive cases, not as rewrites of existing cases.** The 32-step prior plan brought the test suite to the six-section marker grammar; its existing cases (happy path, missing pair, marker order, nesting, malformed marker, deprecated fields, parity, verbatim invariant, name-as-metadata) are all still correct under the broadened args contract. The new behavior — null-armed parsing, null-passthrough through interpolateArgs, null structural round-trip — gets new describe blocks layered on top. This split keeps the diff readable and prevents test-name drift in the existing fixtures.

- **No transitional read path. No literal-sentinel awareness. No auto-coerce.** Per the spec's "YAML null is the unwired-slot signal; the parser preserves it verbatim and never introspects literal-string sentinels" Strategy paragraph and the cluster of undesired outcomes around sentinels, coercion, dropping nulls, and rejecting nulls — every form of "let's just turn nulls into something easier to work with" is rejected. The parser hands nulls through faithfully, the helper hands them through faithfully, the consumer plans (sync, buildBrainState, etc.) decide what to do with nulls per their own contracts. This plan locks in that boundary at the parse layer; the consumer plans land next and consume what this plan delivers.

- **Consumer migrations are explicitly OUT OF SCOPE for this plan.** Per the parent rework's pipelined-work order, consumer plans (`provisionBrain`, `tools/brain`, `cli/sync`, `buildBrainState`, `cli/init`) land separately and consume the broadened type this plan delivers. Step 4's compile/test sweep documents the EXPECTED red errors in consumer modules so the implementor does not get tempted to "just fix one quick thing" outside the parser slice — those fixes belong in their own plans, where the architect can evaluate the consumer's own contracts and tests against the spec.

- **The single-helper file layout still applies.** Per the spec's "Single-helper file layout" Strategy paragraph, the module fits comfortably under ~200 lines (parser + extractMarkerSections inline + interpolateArgs + JSDoc). The args broadening adds no new helpers, no new pipelines, no new responsibilities. `extractMarkerSections` stays inline above the default export per the function-ordering rule. The orchestrator/helper pattern's "extract into subfolders" trigger is "when a helper supports an orchestrator" — `extractMarkerSections` is the orchestrator's body-walking step but the rest of the module's logic (frontmatter validation, deprecated-field rejection, the broadened args validation, interpolation) is inline by spec design and stays that way.
